## 1 Introduction

Neutron stars (NSs) are born in supernovae which are likely anisotropic (e.g., Janka & Muller, 1994; Burrows et al., 1995; Herant, 1995), causing them to receive natal kick velocities (e.g., Van den Heuvel & Van Paradijs, 1997) with magnitudes of \\sim100-1000 km s{}^{-1}(Burrows et al., 2024). NSs observed as isolated pulsars are indeed known to have higher velocities than their progenitors (e.g., Gunn & Ostriker, 1970; Lyne & Lorimer, 1994), but the distribution of the kick velocities that these objects receive remains a topic of discussion. Several studies have tried to constrain the natal kicks of NSs based on the observed properties of isolated pulsars (e.g., Arzoumanian et al., 2002; Hobbs et al., 2005; Faucher-Giguere & Kaspi, 2006; Verbunt et al., 2017; Igoshev, 2020; Disberg et al., 2025), whereas other studies have investigated the natal kicks of NSs in binaries (e.g., Fortin et al., 2022; Zhao et al., 2023). These empirical natal kick distributions are often used in population synthesis models of binary evolution.

In particular, Hobbs et al. (2005) used the proper motion of young isolated pulsars to infer the three-dimensional velocities that they are a projection of. Hobbs et al. (2005) argued that these are well-described by a Maxwellian distribution with \\sigma=265 km s{}^{-1} (peaking at a three-dimensional speed of \\sim400 km s{}^{-1}). Since these pulsars are young (i.e., younger than 3 Myr) their velocities are indicative of the kick magnitude.

However, Verbunt & Cator (2017) argue that there are systematic errors in the distance estimates used by Hobbs et al. (2005) that are based on dispersion measures (DM) and electron density models (e.g., Cordes & Chernoff, 1998; Cordes & Lazio, 2002; Yao et al., 2017), which affect their velocity estimates. Indeed, they show that the transverse velocities of the pulsars analyzed by Brisken et al. (2002) are difficult to explain with the kick distribution of Hobbs et al. (2005).

For this reason, Verbunt et al. (2017) inferred the three-dimensional velocities of pulsars that have distance estimates determined through parallax (as opposed to DM), and found that these velocities are best described by a weighted sum of two Maxwellians. However, they note that this does not necessarily mean that the kick distribution is truly bimodal, since it could also be explained by a distribution that is wider than a single Maxwellian. Igoshev (2020), in turn, redid the analysis of Verbunt et al. (2017) after significantly expanding their pulsar sample with the parallax estimates of Deller et al. (2019), and also found a kick distribution best described by two Maxwellians (see also Igoshev et al., 2021), similarly to the results of Verbunt et al. (2017). The kick distributions of Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020) favor significantly lower velocities than the Maxwellian found by Hobbs et al. (2005).

Recently, Disberg et al. (2024a) investigated the transverse and three-dimensional velocities of pulsars, and found that older pulsars have lower velocities since their kicks cause their Galactic trajectories to be more eccentric (cf. Hansen and Phinney, 1997). Subsequently, Disberg et al. (2024b) used the fact that larger kicks lead to more eccentric Galactic trajectories to determine the systemic kicks of BNSs. That is, they estimated the eccentricities of Galactic BNSs and used a simulation of kicked objects moving in the Galactic potential to determine the relationship between kick magnitude and eccentricity, from which the (systemic) kicks of the BNSs could be inferred (cf. the method of Atri et al., 2019).

This method was expanded by Disberg et al. (2025) and applied to the pulsar samples--including distance estimates--of Igoshev (2020), Verbunt et al. (2017), and Hobbs et al. (2005). While they could recreate the findings of Igoshev (2020) and Verbunt et al. (2017) for young pulsars (including bimodality), they found that their results for the (young) sample of Hobbs et al. (2005) contained significantly lower kicks than predicted by the Maxwellian with \\sigma=265 km s{}^{-1}, instead following a distribution more similar to the other samples. Moreover, taking into account both young and older pulsars, Disberg et al. (2025) found that the NS kicks were best described by a log-normal distribution, and conjectured that the bimodality found for young pulsars might be caused by a limited sample size. Since Disberg et al. (2025) used the DM distance estimates of Hobbs et al. (2005), they argued it is difficult to explain the tension between the Maxwellian kick distribution of Hobbs et al. (2005) and the findings of Verbunt et al. (2017), Igoshev (2020), and Disberg et al. (2025) with systematic errors in the distance estimates.

In this work, we use the largest available sample of both young and older pulsars with either DM or parallax distances. We (1) analyze the current velocities of young pulsars, (2) use the method proposed by Disberg et al. (2024b)--and employed by Disberg et al. (2025)--to kinematically constrain the kicks of old pulsars, (3) investigate the tension in the literature, and (4) fit a consistent model to the distribution of pulsar velocities. In Section 2, we describe the method used to analyze the kicks of the pulsars in our sample, through distance estimates based on parallax or DM. We use the parallax distances in particular to determine a fiducial kick distribution, which we show in Section 3. In Section 4, we investigate the Maxwellian distribution found by Hobbs et al. (2005) and argue that it is likely based on an erroneous histogram interpretation due to a missing Jacobian. We apply our method to the pulsar samples of Igoshev (2020), Verbunt et al. (2017), and Hobbs et al. (2005), and in Section 5 we show that all observations are consistent with our fiducial kick distribution. Finally, in Section 6 we summarize our conclusions.

## 2 Method

We are interested in analyzing the current three-dimensional velocities of young pulsars and applying the method of Disberg et al. (2024b, 2025) to old pulsars. In particular, we consider the pulsars from the Australia Telescope National Facility (ATNF) Pulsar Catalogue1v2.6.0 (Manchester et al., 2005), and only select pulsars with (1) a distance estimate through DM which does not exceed 10 kpc, (2) a proper motion estimate, (3) a characteristic spin-down age estimate (i.e., \\tau_{\\rm c}=1/2\\cdot P/\\dot{P}), (4) \\dot{P}>5\\cdot 10^{-18}, to exclude recycled pulsars, (5) no evidence for the pulsar being part of a binary system, (6) no association with a globular cluster, and (7) no classification as either an "Anomalous X-ray Pulsar or Soft Gamma-ray Repeater with detected pulsations" (AXP) or an "Isolated Neutron Star with pulsed thermal X-ray emission but no detectable radio emission" (XINS). This selection results in a sample of 197 pulsars with DM distance estimates, of which 76 have parallax distance estimates. These parallax estimates update and expand the pulsar sample used by Igoshev (2020)--which contains the results of Chatterjee et al. (2001), Brisken et al. (2002), Brisken et al. (2003), Chatterjee et al. (2004), Chatterjee et al. (2009), Deller et al. (2009), Kirsten et al. (2015), and Deller et al. (2019)--with the measurements of Bruzewski et al. (2023), Lin et al. (2023), and Keith et al. (2024). Moreover, in Appendix A we show the timing parameters (i.e., P and \\dot{P}) of the pulsars in our sample and the distribution of their characteristic ages.

Footnote 1: [https://www.atnf.csiro.au/research/pulsar/psrcat/](https://www.atnf.csiro.au/research/pulsar/psrcat/).

In order to describe the probability distribution for a parallax distance estimate (i.e., D_{\\pi}) corresponding to a certain observed parallax (i.e., \\omega_{\\rm obs}) and its uncertainty (i.e., \\sigma_{\\omega}), we use the formalism proposed by Verbunt & Cator (2017) and employed by Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020). That is, the posterior distribution of D_{\\pi} given an observed parallax \\omega_{\\rm obs} is given by (see also Lorimer et al., 2006; Verbiest et al., 2012; Igoshev et al., 2016):

p(D_{\\pi}\\,|\\,\\omega_{\\rm obs})\\propto\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\left(\\left[ D_{\\pi}/{\\rm kpc}\\right]^{-1}-\\left[\\omega_{\\rm obs}/{\\rm mas}\\right]\\right)^{2}}{2 \\left[\\sigma_{\\omega}/{\\rm mas}\\right]^{2}}\\right)\\\\ \\times D_{\\pi}^{2}R^{1.9}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{|z|}{0.33~{}{\\rm kpc}} -\\frac{R}{1.70~{}{\\rm kpc}}\\right),  where z and R are the galactocentric cylindrical coordinates of the pulsar, where z=D_{\\pi}\\sin b and R^{2}=R_{\\odot}^{2}+\\left(D_{\\pi}\\cos b\\right)^{2}-2R_{\\odot}D_{\\pi}\\cos b \\cos l for Galactic coordinates l, b and galactocentric radius of the Solar System R_{\\odot}=8.122 kpc (GRAVITY Collab. et al., 2018). We determine the parallax distances (for each parallax pulsar) by sampling 10^{3} values from Equation 1, whereas the DM distances (for all pulsars) are estimated by sampling 10^{3} values from a normal distribution centered around the distance determined through the electron density model of Yao et al. (2017), with a standard deviation of 20%. Although detailed studies such as those of Deller et al. (2009) show that DM distances can sometimes have even greater systematic errors, we use an indicative error of 20% (e.g. Ding et al., 2024). We note that we primarily use DM distance estimates to show that these are not sufficient to explain the differences between the results of Verbunt et al. (2017) and those of Hobbs et al. (2005).

Having obtained 10^{3} DM distance samples for all 197 pulsars and 10^{3} parallax distances for the 76 pulsars with parallax estimates, we also sample 10^{3} proper motion samples from Gaussians based on the observed values and their uncertainties. Then, we convert the coordinates to the galactocentric frame (essentially adding the velocity of the Solar System), and project the resulting velocity vectors on the line of sight and the direction perpendicular to the line of sight. The latter we define as the galactocentric transverse velocity v_{t} (see also Gaspari et al., 2024). If the galactocentric velocity vector \\vec{v} is projected on the line of sight and the plane of the sky, it can be described as:

\\vec{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}v_{\\alpha}\\\\ v_{\\delta}\\\\ v_{r}\\end{pmatrix}=v\\begin{pmatrix}\\sin\\phi\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\cos\\phi\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix},  where the transverse velocity equals v_{t}=\\sqrt{v_{\\alpha}^{2}+v_{\\delta}^{2}}=v\\sin\\theta and the radial velocity equals v_{r}=v\\cos\\theta (note that "radial" here refers to the line of sight, rather than the Galactic radial coordinate). If this vector is oriented isotropically in the galactocentric frame (GC isotropy), then \\phi is distributed uniformly and p(\\theta)=1/2\\cdot\\sin\\theta for 0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi. This means that for GC isotropy, the radial component of the velocity vector is given by

v_{r}=v_{t}\\cot\\theta\\quad\\left(\\text{GC isotropy}\\right),  where \\theta=\\arccos u (with u being uniformly sampled between -1 and 1), since p(\\theta)\\mathrm{d}\\theta=p(u)\\mathrm{d}u and p(u)=p(-u)=1/2. However, if the velocity vector is oriented isotropically in the local standard of rest (LSR) of the pulsar, the galactocentric radial velocity is (Gaspari et al., 2024):

v_{r}=||\\vec{v}_{t}-\\vec{v}_{\\rm LSR,\\,t}||\\cot\\theta+v_{\\rm LSR,\\,r}\\quad \\left(\\text{LSR isotropy}\\right),  where \\vec{v}_{\\rm LSR,\\,t} and v_{\\rm LSR,\\,r} describe the transverse and radial velocities of the LSR of the pulsar relative to the Solar System.

We combine the distance estimates with the proper motions to create samples of the transverse velocity for each pulsar, and then use Equation 4 to create a corresponding sample for the radial velocity distribution, since LSR isotropy is a more accurate assumption than GC isotropy (Disberg et al., 2024). The transverse and radial velocity samples form the three-dimensional velocity vectors in the galactocentric frame, which we transformed to velocities relative to the pulsars' LSR by subtracting \\vec{v}_{\\rm LSR}. We approximate v_{\\rm LSR} by using the circular velocity at the location of the pulsar (as given by the Milky Way potential of McMillan, 2017). As a result we obtain the present-day velocities of the pulsars relative to their LSR--which we define as "v_{\\rm pd}". For pulsars with characteristic spin-down ages \\tau_{\\rm c} below 10 Myr, we approximate their kicks by estimating v_{\\rm pd}, since for pulsars with these ages their current velocities are still representative of their kicks (Disberg et al., 2024). Our sample contains 142 pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}<10 Myr, of which 48 have parallax estimates. Moreover, although there are cases in which \\tau_{\\rm c} does not appear to be accurate (e.g., Lyne et al., 1996 find a factor 2-3 difference between \\tau_{\\rm c} and their age estimates), it tends to agree with kinematic age estimates (e.g., Noutsos et al., 2013; Igoshev, 2019; Disberg et al., 2025) and in particular Maoz & Nakar (2025) argue that it is a reasonable age estimate.

For pulsars older than 10 Myr, their present-day velocities have increasingly been affected by their migration through the Galactic potential (Disberg et al., 2024), which is why we employ the method of Disberg et al. (2025) to estimate the kicks of these older pulsars. That is, we sample 10^{2} velocity vectors for each of these pulsars, similarly to the young ones, trace their trajectoriesback through the Galaxy (with GALPY2 v1.10.0, Bovy 2015), and estimate the eccentricity of their Galactic orbits, since eccentricity is a good indicator of kick magnitude even for older pulsars (Disberg et al., 2024). Then, we use the simulated relationship between eccentricity and kick magnitude found by Disberg et al. (2025, see their Figure 4). This relationship describes the Galactic eccentricities of kicked objects within the solar neighborhood (defined as distances below 2 kpc), integrated over a given time domain. For each of the 100 eccentricity values per pulsar we sample 10 kick velocities from the relationship between kick velocity and Galactic eccentricity, while assuming a flat prior on the kicks, and obtain a distribution of kinematically constrained kick velocities--which we define as "v_{\\rm kc}"--where we correct for detection bias by considering the fraction of objects that cross the solar neighborhood as a function of kick magnitude.

Footnote 2: [http://github.com/jobovy/galpy](http://github.com/jobovy/galpy).

The benefit of this method is that it can be used to obtain information about the kicks of old objects, whereas previous methods used to determine NS kicks only analyzed the current velocities of young objects. However, the downside of this method is that this model makes several assumptions that bring in more systematic uncertainties than the method used for young pulsars. For example, we integrate the eccentricity versus kick relationship over a time domain and compare it to pulsars with ages within this domain, but this relationship evolves over time and the distribution of pulsar ages is generally not uniform within a given time domain (see Appendix A). The boundaries of the time domains are sensitive to the accuracy of \\tau_{\\rm c} as an approximation for the pulsar age. We assume a static Milky Way model because Galactic evolution since the formation of the pulsars is not significant (e.g. Amend et al., 2024). Moreover, our model of the detection probability of a kicked neutron star, through the probability that it reaches (our definition of) the solar neighborhood, is an approximation that brings in systematic uncertainty as well. Because of these systematic model uncertainties, we base our fiducial kick distribution only on the young pulsars (i.e., \\tau_{\\rm c}<10 Myr).

## 3 Results

We divide our pulsar sample into four age ranges: \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 3 Myr (similarly to Hobbs et al., 2005; Igoshev, 2020), \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr (similarly to Verbunt et al., 2017), 10 Myr <\\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 40 Myr, and \\tau_{\\rm c}>40 Myr, and sample 10^{3} present-day velocity values for the pulsars younger than 10 Myr and 10^{3} kinematically constrained kick values for the ones older than 10 Myr. We fit a log-normal distribution (similarly to Disney et al., 2025) to the kick distributions for each age range:

p_{\\rm ln}(v\\,|\\,\\mu,\\sigma)=\\frac{1}{v\\sigma\\,\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{ \\left(\\ln v-\\mu\\right)^{2}}{2\\sigma^{2}}\\right),  which we renormalize for the velocity range 0\\leq v\\leq 1000 km s{}^{-1}. The likelihood of obtaining the set of observations given parameters \\mu and \\sigma is (see Mandel et al., 2019):

\\mathcal{L}\\left(\\mu,\\sigma\\,|\\,\\vec{v}\\right)=\\prod_{n=1}^{N}\\frac{1}{10^{3} }\\sum_{i=1}^{10^{3}}\\frac{p_{\\rm ln}(\\vec{v}_{n,i}\\,|\\,\\mu,\\sigma)}{\\int p_{ \\rm det}(v)\\,p_{\\rm ln}(v\\,|\\mu,\\sigma)\\,{\\rm d}v},  where \\vec{v} is the vector containing the resulting velocity values such that \\vec{v}_{n,i} is the i^{\\rm th} sample for the n^{\\rm th} pulsar (for a data set containing N pulsars), and p_{\\rm det}(v) is the probability of detecting a pulsar that received a kick equal to v, which is assumed to be uniform for young pulsars and for old pulsars is determined by the simulation of Disberg et al. (2025, see their Figure 4). We fit the log-normal distribution by finding the values of \\mu and \\sigma for which the likelihood \\mathcal{L} is maximal.

In Table 1 we list the log-normal parameters (i.e., \\mu and \\sigma) fitted to pulsar velocities in the various age ranges. The younger the pulsars, the more representative their current velocities are for their natal kicks, but in order to limit the effects of low number statistics we

