Table 1: Parameters of the log-normal fits (Equations 5 and 6) to the velocity distributions of the pulsars in our sample. consider the fit to pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr (based on parallax distances), which results in \\mu=5.60\\pm 0.12 and \\sigma=0.68\\pm 0.10, to be our fiducial kick distribution. For the oldest pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}>40 Myr, we find the data to be insufficient for constraining \\mu and \\sigma. However, through Equation 6 we are able to fit a log-normal distribution to the pulsars with ages between 10 and 40 Myr, and by multiplying the likelihood for this sample with the likelihood for pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr we constrain the parameters even further and obtain a fit for the pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 40 Myr, which results in \\mu=5.67\\pm 0.10 and \\sigma=0.59\\pm 0.08 (for parallax distance estimates).

The statistical uncertainties on \\mu and \\sigma quoted in Table 1 are half the width of the 68% credible intervals on these quantities obtained from the posterior on \\mu and \\sigma assuming the likelihood function of Equation 6 and flat priors. We note that for parallax distances our parameter estimates are consistent within their error bars, though the estimated \\sigma for the 10<\\tau_{\\rm c}/{\\rm Myr}\\leq 40 sample is a marginal outlier. Table 1 also lists fits based on DM distance estimates, but we are more confident in the distance estimates based on parallax. The parallax estimates are marginally consistent with the parameter estimates for DM distances, which favor smaller \\mu and wider \\sigma.

In Figure 1 we show log-normal fits for the DM distances and the parallax distances, together with histograms of the most likely velocity values for each pulsar (after reweighting by our fiducial kick distribution as the prior). The top row shows the velocity estimates of young pulsars (for \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 3 Myr and \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr), relative to the LSR of the pulsar, assuming LSR isotropy (Equation 4). These distributions appear to be well-described by our fiducial kick distribution as well as our combined fit for all pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 40 Myr, both of which peak at \\sim150-200 km s{}^{-1}. Both of these are in general agreement with the distribution found by Disney et al. (2025), although the current analysis is able to better constrain the high-velocity tail of the distribution. Panel B shows the corresponding velocity distributions based on the DM distances, which contain slightly lower velocities than the parallax-based velocities. These distributions still follow log-normal distributions, which peak at \\sim100 km/s.

The second row of Figure 1 shows the kinematically constrained kick velocities for the older pulsars. The resulting distributions generally agree with the velocity distribution for the young pulsars: they are centered around \\sim200 km s{}^{-1} for the parallax distances, and contain slightly lower kicks for the DM distances. The log-normal fit to the pulsars with ages between 10 Myr and 40 Myr is in general agreement with our fiducial kick distribution: it peaks at \\sim200 km s{}^{-1}.

The last row of Figure 1 shows cumulative distribution functions (CDFs) of the velocity estimates for pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr and the kinematically constrained kick estimates for 10<\\tau_{\\rm c}/\\rm Myr\\leq 40. The 95% observational confidence intervals are obtained by bootstrapping: we randomly select (with replacement) N pulsars from the data set and for each pulsar select one velocity sample to create a single observational CDF, then repeat this 10^{3} times and determine the 95% intervals of the resulting set of empirical CDFs. For the parallax distance estimates (panel E), both the fiducial kick distribution and the fit for all pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 40 Myr lie within the confidence intervals for both young and older pulsars, despite the fact that the older pulsar sample is likely biased against high kicks. A log-normal distribution is a sufficient model for the kicks of the pulsars in our data set, and we find no evidence for a statistically significant bimodality (cf. Verbunt et al., 2017; Igoshev, 2020) or multimodality (cf. Valli et al., 2025) in the kick distribution. It is unsurprising that the log-normal fit does not trace the median velocity distribution perfectly because the velocity distributions for the individual pulsars are asymmetrically skewed to higher values, mainly due to the projection factor of \\cot\\theta in Equations 3 and 4, as we show in Appendix B.

In our fiducial kick distribution only \\sim1% of pulsars obtain low kicks (below 50 km s{}^{-1}, following Willcox et al., 2021), which are relevant for retaining NSs in globular clusters or wide binaries, although in the 95% confidence region this fraction may be as large as \\sim10%. It seems likely that there is a subpopulation of NSs that are formed following binary interactions, receive low natal kicks, therefore remain in binaries such as low-eccentricity Be X-ray binaries (e.g., Valli et al., 2025), wide low-eccentricity detached NS binaries observed with Gaia (though post-natal kick mechanisms could be at play, see Hirai et al., 2024) and in clusters (e.g., Smits et al., 2006), and are thus explicitly excluded from our observational isolated pulsar data set. These might, for example, originate from electron-capture supernovae that happen predominantly or exclusively for progenitors stripped by mass transfer and thus avoiding second dredge-up (Willcox et al., 2021) or from accretion induced collapse. Another possibility to consider is that NSs that receive low kicks are somehow different from typical pulsars and avoid pulsar surveys unless they are recycled, but there is no evidence for a correlation between kick velocity and pulsar detectability in the existing data (Willcox et al., 2021).

For the DM distances (panel F), the fiducial kick distribution lies slightly outside of the confidence interval for the young pulsars, since parallax distance estimates tend to exceed DM distances. However, we are more confident in the parallax distance estimates, which is why our fiducial kick distribution is solely based on the parallax distance estimates. In Appendix C, we compare the distance and velocity estimates based on DM and parallax.

## 4 Maxwellian

Because parallax velocities tend to exceed DM velocities, it is difficult to argue that the fact that Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020) find velocities lower than the ones found by Hobbs et al. (2005) is caused by the difference between parallax and DM distances (as also found by Disberg et al., 2025). Also, Valli et al. (2025) find that the kick distribution of Hobbs et al. (2005) is strongly disfavored for explaining the properties of their Be X-ray binary sample. The velocity estimates of Hobbs et al. (2005) are based on an analysis of the proper motions and are inferred by applying a CLEAN-type algorithm (Hogbom, 1974) to the estimated transverse velocities of their pulsar sample (for \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 3 Myr). To their results they fitted a Maxwellian distribution:

p_{\\mbox{\\tiny{M}}}(v\\,|\\,\\sigma)=\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}}\\frac{v^{2}}{\\sigma^{3} }\\exp\\left(\\frac{-v^{2}}{2\\sigma^{2}}\\right),  and found that \\sigma=265 km s{}^{-1} fits their resulting histogram well. Their Maxwellian fit has a median \\sim50% larger than our fiducial kick distribution and thus predicts significantly higher velocities. In an attempt to recreate their results, we consider their values of v_{t} (see their Table 1) which are based on DM distances through the electron density model of Cordes & Lazio (2002), for pulsars with \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 3 Myr (see their Table 2). Then, we follow Hobbs et al. (2005) in assuming GC isotropy and estimate the unknown radial velocity by correcting for Solar motion, sampling 10^{3} values from Equation 3, and subtracting the velocity of the LSR of the pulsar, in order to sample three-dimensional velocities of the pulsars relative to their LSR.

In panel A of Figure 2 we show a histogram of the transverse velocities listed by Hobbs et al. (2005, in their Table 1) in uniform logarithmic bins, as they have done. The height of the histogram bins are not corrected for the logarithmic bin sizes, and since these bin sizes are proportional to v this means that the histogram is effectively showing

p(\\log v)=\\frac{p(v){\\rm d}v}{{\\rm d}\\log v}\\propto v\\,p(v)\\propto\\mbox{bin size}\\cdot p(v). 

In other words, this histogram is missing the Jacobian {\\rm d}v/{\\rm d}\\log v and therefore shows p(\\log v) instead of p(v). We find that our histogram of v_{t} that excludes this Jacobian is identical to the histogram of Hobbs et al. (2005, see their Figure 4b).

Similarly, if we describe the distribution of three-dimensional velocities with a histogram that does not include a Jacobian and thus shows p(\\log v), the result is similar to the histogram of Hobbs et al. (2005, see their Figure 7). However, to fit the histogrammed empirical distribution to the p(v) Maxwellian of Equation 7, a Jacobian must be included. We replot the histogram in uniform v bins in panel B of Figure 2, including the Jacobian. It is apparent that the empirical distribution contains significantly lower velocities than predicted by the Hobbs et al. (2005) Maxwellian and is instead well-described by our log-normal fiducial distribution (despite the fact that these velocities are determined assuming GC isotropy instead of LSR isotropy).

Based on our analysis shown in Figure 2, we conclude that the Jacobian that corrects for the logarithmic bin sizes is missing from the analysis of Hobbs et al. (2005), meaning that their Maxwellian fit is inconsistent with the data and significantly overestimates the true kick distribution. This explains why Disney et al. (2025) are not able to reproduce this Maxwellian, but instead find a distribution more similar to our fiducial kick distribution. The difference between the bimodal kick distributions found by Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020) and the Maxwellian of Hobbs et al. (2005) is therefore not due to inaccuracy in DM distance estimates, but is caused by an erroneous histogram representation due to a missing Jacobian.

## 5 Comparison

In order to compare our results to the velocity distributions found by Hobbs et al. (2005), Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020), we plot the histograms of inferred velocities of the young pulsars in their samples (i.e., for \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 3 Myr as well as \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr) following our methodology described in Section 2 (cf. Figure 1). In panels A, B, and C of Figure 3 we overplot these histograms with the distributions inferred by the respective previous analyses; the histograms for the samples of Igoshev (2020) and Verbunt et al. (2017) resemble their bimodal distributions while the Hobbs et al. (2005) data do not match their Maxwellian because of the Jacobian error. That is, for the Igoshev sample there appears to be a bimodality for \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 3 Myr and for the Verbunt sample there seems to be a bimodality for both age ranges (as also found by Disberg et al., 2025). In Appendix D we show that we can reproduce their double-Maxwellian fits, defined as

p_{\\mbox{\\tiny DM}}(v\\,|\\,\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2},w)=w\\,p_{\\mbox{\\tiny M}}(v\\,| \\,\\sigma_{1})+(1-w)\\,p_{\\mbox{\\tiny M}}(v\\,|\\,\\sigma_{2}),  where p_{\\mbox{\\tiny M}}(v\\,|\\,\\sigma) is a Maxwellian distribution (Equation 7), by applying our methodology to their pulsar velocity sample. For the Hobbs sample, we use the Galactic coordinates and proper motions listed in their Table 2 and assume LSR isotropy (Equation 4) as opposed to GC isotropy (Equation 3). We are therefore more confident in these velocity estimates than in the ones shown in Figure 2, and they follow a log-normal distribution well-described by our fiducial kick distribution.

In panel D of Figure 3, we show CDFs of the present-day velocity distributions of these pulsar samples, for \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr. We estimate the 95% confidence intervals by bootstrapping the distributions, similarly to our main results shown in Figure 1. We also show our fidu cial kick distribution as well as our fit for \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 40 Myr, both of which are encompassed by all three confidence intervals. We therefore argue that (1) correcting the results of Hobbs et al. (2005) for the logarithmic bin sizes reconciles them with the results of Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020), and (2) our fiducial kick distribution is compatible with all of these NS kicks analyses. Nevertheless, we do note that for larger sample sizes we expect the confidence regions of velocity distributions based on DM distances to be slightly below our kick distribution (as shown in Figures 1).

Moreover, despite the fact that there are good reasons why the kick distribution may be bimodal (see e.g. Katz, 1975; Fryer et al., 1998; Podsiadlowski et al., 2004; Fryer and Young, 2007; Banerjee et al., 2020; Willcox et al., 2021; Disberg et al., 2025; Valli et al., 2025, and references therein), the bimodality found by Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020) is not statistically significant: the distributions are well described by our unimodal log-normal fiducial kick distribution. The bimodality is therefore likely a result of low-number statistics. In order to examine this, we randomly select 21 pulsars (the number of young pulsars used in the Igoshev 2020 analysis) from our sample of \\tau_{\\rm c}\\leq 10 Myr parallax pulsars and evaluate the histogrammed velocity PDFs. We find that the PDF drops below 1.2\\cdot 10^{-3} s km{}^{-1} in the 200-250 km s{}^{-1} in 10% of the 1000 random draws of 21 pulsars, showing a bimodality similar to the distributions of Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020). Lastly, we investigate whether a distribution such as a Maxwellian or a (bimodal) double-Maxwellian could provide a better fit to the data than our log-normal model. As shown in Appendix E, we do not find strong evidence in favor of an alternative kick distribution.

## 6 Conclusions

We have used the proper motions and distances (both based on parallax and DM) of young pulsars in the ATNF Pulsar Catalogue (Manchester et al., 2005) and estimated their present-day velocities relative to their LSR by assuming LSR isotropy (Equation 4). Moreover, we have compared our results to the works of Hobbs et al. (2005), Verbunt et al. (2017), and Igoshev (2020). Based on our findings, we conclude the following:

* The present-day velocities of pulsars younger than 10 Myr, relative to their LSR, are well-described by a log-normal distribution (Equation 5) with \\mu=5.60\\pm 0.12 and \\sigma=0.69\\pm 0.10, which we adopt as our fiducial kick distribution (Table 1 and Figure 1).

* The kicks of pulsars older than 10 Myr, kinematically constrained using the method of Disberg et al. (2025), also show a single peak at \\sim200 km s{}^{-1}, similar to the velocities of young pulsars. If we include the inferred kick velocities of pulsars with ages between 10 Myr and 40 Myr in our fit, we find a more tightly constrained log-normal distribution with \\mu=5.67\\pm 0.10 and \\sigma=0.59\\pm 0.08, which is, however, more likely to suffer from systematics.

* The velocities based on DM distances are slightly below these results, and therefore cannot explain the difference between the Maxwellian distribution found by Hobbs et al. (2005) and the double-Maxwellians found by Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020).

* Hobbs et al. (2005) erroneously compared an empirical velocity distribution p(\\log v) against a model p(v) without including a Jacobian correction. Therefore, their Maxwellian fit is incorrect and significantly overestimates the true velocities (Figure 2).

* Our method can reproduce the double-Maxwellian distributions of Verbunt et al. (2017) and Igoshev (2020) relatively accurately. However, we argue that the bimodality that they find is not statistically significant but likely caused by low-number statistics, and our fiducial kick distribution is consistent with their results as well as the results of Hobbs et al. (2005) that are corrected for the missing Jacobian (Figure 3).

The method we use to estimate the present-day pulsar velocities makes several assumptions. For instance, our fiducial kick distribution does not take into account a likely binary origin of the pulsars, in which the pulsars are kicked out of the binary system (e.g., Kuranov et al., 2009; Beniamini and Piran, 2024). This binary origin can impact the evolutionary history: for example, binary interactions stripping supernova progenitors may affect kick magnitudes (Muller et al., 2018; Willcox et al., 2021). Also, the pre-supernova orbital velocity of the binary will scatter the speed of the ejected single pulsar around the kick magnitude. Perhaps most importantly, binarity imposes a selection effect: only sufficiently large kicks will unbind binaries, potentially leading to an over-representation of high kicks among apparently single pulsars. However, Kapil et al. (2023) find that a binary origin does not significantly influence observed kicks in their population-synthesis models. Moreover, we neglect the effects of overdensities in the Galactic disc on the velocities of kicked objects (Ofek, 2009) or the potential effect of a "rocket"-like acceleration due to spin-downradiation (Hirai et al., 2024). Lastly, we also do not consider a potential observational bias caused by spin-kick alignment (Biryukov & Beskin, 2024), which may cause pulsar velocities to be overestimated by \\sim15% (Mandel & Igoshev, 2023).

